        {
          "title": "Rams",
          "subtitle": "Trick-taking",
          "content": [
            {
              "type": "paragraph",
              "text": "Rams is a European trick-taking game family related to Nap and Loo, often played as a gambling or drinking game with optional entry into each hand."
            },
            {
              "type": "paragraph",
              "text": "Published rules appear in France around 1820, with related Austrian and Bavarian evidence from the same period and later spread to Sweden and America as Rounce."
            }
          ]
        },
        {
          "title": "Setup",
          "subtitle": "Table, deck, equipment, and deal",
          "content": [
            {
              "type": "paragraph",
              "text": "3-9 players use the table arrangement described for Rams."
            },
            {
              "type": "paragraph",
              "text": "Usually 32-card piquet or regional equivalent; variants use 52 cards"
            },
            {
              "type": "paragraph",
              "text": "Usually 32-card piquet or regional equivalent; variants use 52 cards, score area, and enough table space for play."
            },
            {
              "type": "paragraph",
              "text": "Prepare Usually 32-card piquet or regional equivalent; variants use 52 cards and deal or lay out cards according to the documented Rams rules before play begins."
            }
          ]
        }
      ]
    }
  ]
